        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Failure of the urethral smooth and/or striated muscle relaxation as the detrusor muscle contracts at the beginning of urination. Thought to be due to dysfunction of the reflex arc allowing urethral sphincter relaxation as the detrusor muscle contracts at the beginning of urination.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "This is a disorder of the emptying phase of micturition due to functional obstruction of the outflow tract and urethra. While the pathophysiology is not completely understood, it is thought that the neurologic lesion is located within the reticulospinal tract, Onuf nucleus, or caudal mesenteric ganglion. The loss of inhibitory signals to the pudendal and hypogastric nerves may also be involved. Involvement of a more local lesion (peripheral nerves, neuromuscular junction, striated and smooth muscles of the sphincters, etc.) is unknown. It is unclear whether anxiety and other behavior disorders may contribute to the disorder.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urinary tract.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Typically have otherwise normal neurologic assessment.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "GENETICS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "GENETICS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "INCIDENCE/PREVALENCE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "INCIDENCE/PREVALENCE",
          "canonical_heading": "INCIDENCE/PREVALENCE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Unknown. The literature suggests increasing incidence which may be related to increased awareness and thus diagnosis in male dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "GEOGRAPHIC DISTRIBUTION": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "GEOGRAPHIC DISTRIBUTION",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dogs (cats can be affected). Most common in middle-aged male dogs (mean age of 4.9 years). Females are also affected although rarely. Large- to giant-breed dogs most commonly affected.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Neurologic dysfunction due to spinal cord lesions cranial to the sacral segment resulting in upper motor neuron bladder signs with simultaneous detrusor and urethral contraction.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Damage to the reflex arc impairing urethral sphincter relaxation during detrusor contraction.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urethral spasm of the smooth or striated muscles.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Controversial behavioral/anxiety-driven component in some patients.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Large- to giant-breed dogs (25–64 kg).",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Young adults.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Intact males—sexual excitement in the presence of intact females.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cauda equina syndrome.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urinary tract infections or urethritis resulting in urethral spasm.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Surgery in the area of the bladder neck.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urethral and prostatic tumors.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Medications). Owners can also be instructed how to intermittently pass sterile catheters at home to address residual urine volume. If",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
