              "text": "Prazosin may cause hypotension, CNS signs unless the animal is actively voiding. such as lethargy or dizziness, weakness, syncope, and gastrointestinal upset.",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tamsulosin may cause hypotension though the safe dosage range is quite wide and severe",
              "pdf_page": 1448,
              "printed_page": 1366,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Prazosin should not be used in conjunction with beta-blockers, sildenafil, or verapamil/nifedipine due to concerns for increased risk of hypotension.",
              "pdf_page": 1449,
              "printed_page": 1367,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tamulosin should not be used with other alpha-1 adrenergic antagonists or sildenafil/tadalafil as they may have cumulative hypotensive effects. Cimetidine, charithromycin, erythromycin, ketoconazole, and terbinafine may all result in increased plasma levels of tamulosin.",
              "pdf_page": 1449,
              "printed_page": 1367,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Acepromazine may have adverse effects when administered in conjunction with a number of drugs. Be careful when",
              "pdf_page": 1449,
              "printed_page": 1367,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "administering with other drugs that may cause CNS effects or hypotension.",
              "pdf_page": 1449,
              "printed_page": 1367,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Diazepam should be used with caution when administered concurrently with other CNS-depressant medications. There are also several classes of drugs that may increase or decrease plasma levels of diazepam when administered concurrently.",
              "pdf_page": 1449,
              "printed_page": 1367,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Methocarbamol should be used with caution when other CNS-depressant medications are also prescribed.",
              "pdf_page": 1449,
              "printed_page": 1367,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bethanechol should not be used with other cholinergic or anticholinergic medications due to synergistic or antagonistic effects, respectively.",
              "pdf_page": 1449,
              "printed_page": 1367,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cisapride’s effects may be decreased when given with anticholinergics. When given with benzodiazepines the sedative effects may be enhanced.",
              "pdf_page": 1449,
              "printed_page": 1367,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
